        "problem": "$\\frac{1}{z}+\\frac{3}{z-3}=$",
        "worked_solution": "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $z(z-3),$ since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit.\n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $z-3$, and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{z}+\\frac{3}{z-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{z-3}{z-3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{z}+\\frac{z}{z}\\cdot \\frac{3}{z-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{z-3}{z(z-3)}+\\frac{3z}{z(z-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{z-3+3z}{z(z-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4z-3}{z(z-3)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
